And therefore listen to this summary: Nārāyaṇa is this entire Purāṇa;
even at the time of conjunction he makes the path, and at the time of dissolution it does not exist again.

Thus ends the first chapter named 'Summary of Contents' in the first quarter, the Prakriyāpāda, in the first part proclaimed by Vāyu in the glorious Brahmāṇḍa Mahāpurāṇa.

Then that Brahmā becomes the thousand-eyed, thousand-footed,
thousand-headed Puruṣa, golden-hued, with subdued senses;
and in this regard they cite this verse concerning Nārāyaṇa:

The great sages beginning with Bhṛgu, as indicated at that time,
and eight great sages beginning with Satya, when the kalpa was dissolved;
then, while being transformed by them, the great [ocean] was completely surrounded by the supreme.

Bṛhaspati said:
These seven troops of Pitṛs in heaven are remembered, best among victors;
four have forms, and three of them are formless.

Śaṃyu said:
‘Tell me, who am asking, father, the fruit of Gayā and the rest;
and tell me in full what merit belongs to the givers.’

Jamadagni said:
"I am never in any way an agent of buying and selling;
therefore, I cannot bear to give away the yielder of oblations straight out."

The minister said:
"Give this single cow in exchange for half the king's realm, or even the whole [of it], O brahmin;
that will be for your welfare."

The minister said:
"Give the cow to the king of your own free will, out of friendship;
otherwise, what will you do when she is carried off by force?"

Kṛṣṇa said:
“Come, O fortunate Rāma, your excellent purpose is accomplished;
know it to be so through the power of the armor and of the hymn.

Śrī Rādhā said:
Prakṛti and Puruṣa are both forms depending mutually on one another;
they appear manifested as divided into two in this manifold world, in that manner.

Vasiṣṭha said:
Proceeding on the path, he heard from sages the truth from the beginning:
the deed of the princes and the departure of his parents to heaven;

The Śyāmāyanas, Hiraṇyākṣas, Sāṅkṛtas, and Gālavas are remembered,
the Devalas, Yāmadūtas, Śālaṅkāyanas, and Bāṣkalas;

The Lālāḍhyas, Bādaras, and others of the wise Viśvāmitra;
these numerous Kauśikas are remembered as marrying into other ṛṣi lineages.

Thus has this dissolution of the elements been declared to you by me.
This withdrawal through washing away has Brahmā as its cause;
it has been declared in brief. What more shall I describe to you?

Devoted, endowed with faith, free from arrogance, who act accordingly,
abiding by the Vedic and traditional duties of the classes and stages of life;
their offices have ceased until the end of the manvantara.

And having worshipped the Supreme Śakti, one is freed from all sins;
by this, sins committed even over ten million aeons are destroyed;
one is released from all calamities and attains every desired object.

The diverse Śaktis, constantly eager for the slaying of the Asura Bhaṇḍa, [advanced];
yet even that circular doorway, extending a yojana in length and width,
was not sufficient for the commander of the army of the fire-rampart circle.

Because of the speed and dexterity of her hand, nothing could be discerned
of the movement of the Mahārājñī’s lotus-hand in discharging arrows;
listen, O pot-born [Agastya], I shall tell you all about the combat.

its length is five yojanas, and its arrangement consists of seven enclosures.
Likewise in the other intermediate directions are priyaka trees on all sides;
this is celebrated as the dwelling city of Śyāmā.

The daily service of the venerable Devī could not be performed by the Mantriṇī [from so far away];
therefore, a mansion was also constructed near the Cintāmaṇi palace
for that venerable mistress of mantras by Tvaṣṭṛ, the artisan of the gods, and by Maya.

Agastya said:
There must surely be guardians of the seven ramparts of iron and the other metals;
proclaim their names, O wise one, so that my doubt may be dispelled.
